{
  "ID": "12",
  "Context/Background": "Renal cell carcinoma with sarcomatoid features (sRCC) is a highly aggressive tumor type yet preferentially responds to immune checkpoint blockade (ICB). sRCC is unique in its ability to develop from various epithelial RCC subtypes, most frequently ccRCC, and sarcomatoid features typically exist as foci nested within an epithelioid RCC. Sarcomatoid clones are thought to arise from transformation of an epithelioid RCC cell through epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT).To better understand microenvironmental mediators of the paradoxical immune sensitivity of sRCC, we performed single-cell analyses of human sRCC tumors compared against clear cell RCC (ccRCC), with validation spatially and in bulk transcriptomic datasets totaling over 3,000 RCC tumors. ",
  "Prompt": "Gene expression counts were aggregated by case and differential expression analysis on pseudobulked counts was performed. Based on the differential gene expression results, run GSEA on hallmark genesets, and provide a brief discussion of pathways important in the sRCC vs ccRCC context. From the top 20 differentially expressed genes, prioritize 5 genes and discuss their possible roles in tumorigenesis and sarcomatoid transformation. ",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "nvidia",
  "llm_model": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b",
  "run_date": "2026-01-20 19:30:17",
  "elapsed_seconds": 141.06,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "### Introduction/Context  \nThe study compares sarcomatoid renal cell carcinoma (sRCC) and clear cell RCC (ccRCC), focusing on pseudobulk DEG data from Salgia et al. (2025). sRCC is characterized by aggressive behavior and paradoxical sensitivity to immune checkpoint blockade, with sarcomatoid transformation hypothesized to involve epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT). The uploaded dataset includes 14,871 genes, with SEMA3C and DCBLD2 as top upregulated DEGs (logFC >3.5, FDR <0.05).  \n\n---\n\n### Keywords  \n- **sarcomatoid transformation**  \n- **epithelial-mesenchymal transition (EMT)**  \n- **immune checkpoint blockade**  \n- **tumor microenvironment**  \n\n### Candidate Genes  \n1. **SEMA3C** (semaphorin 3C)  \n2. **DCBLD2** (discoidin domain receptor-like)  \n3. **TFAP2A** (transcription factor AP-2α)  \n4. **NPR1** (natriuretic peptide receptor 1)  \n5. **MOXD1** (monooxygenase DBH-like 1)  \n\n---\n\n### Pathway Enrichments  \n**MSigDB Hallmark Analysis**:  \n- **Upregulated (9 pathways)**: Dominated by EMT (NES=5.45), KRAS signaling down (NES=3.11), myogenesis (NES=2.99), and apical junction (NES=2.98).  \n- **Downregulated (2 pathways)**: TNF-α signaling via NF-κB (NES=-2.93) and Myc targets V2 (NES=-2.04).  \n\n| Hallmark Pathway               | Direction | Leading Genes (e.g.)                     |  \n|-------------------------------|-----------|------------------------------------------|  \n| **Epithelial Mesenchymal Transition** | Up        | CD44, COL1A1, FN1, VCAN, POSTN, TIMP1    |  \n| **KRAS Signaling Dn**         | Up        | IGFBP2, TFAP2B, CLDN8, EGF, TGFB2        |  \n| **TNF-α Signaling via NF-κB** | Down      | NFKBIA, IL6, ICAM1, TNFAIP3, RELB        |  \n\n**Key Insight**: EMT and mesenchymal features dominate sRCC, while downregulation of NF-κB signaling suggests reduced inflammatory signaling compared to ccRCC.  \n\n---\n\n### Targeted Pathway Analysis  \n- **EMT Pathway**: The top upregulated pathway (NES=5.45) includes 31 overlapping DEGs (e.g., CD44, COL1A1, FN1), consistent with sarcomatoid transformation’s mesenchymal phenotype.  \n- **TNF-α Signaling**: Downregulated in sRCC (NES=-2.93), with reduced expression of pro-inflammatory genes (e.g., IL6, ICAM1), potentially linking immune sensitivity to a less inflammatory microenvironment.  \n\n---\n\n### Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge)  \n**Upregulated in EMT Pathway**:  \n| Gene    | Role/Function                          | Pathway(s)                    |  \n|---------|----------------------------------------|-------------------------------|  \n| **CD44**| Cell adhesion, EMT marker              | EMT, Apical Junction          |  \n| **COL1A1**| Extracellular matrix remodeling       | EMT, Myogenesis               |  \n| **FN1** | Cell adhesion and migration            | EMT, Angiogenesis             |  \n| **POSTN**| Mesenchymal cell adhesion             | EMT, Angiogenesis             |  \n\n**Downregulated in TNF-α Pathway**:  \n| Gene     | Role/Function                         | Pathway(s)                    |  \n|----------|---------------------------------------|-------------------------------|  \n| **IL6**  | Pro-inflammatory cytokine             | TNF-α Signaling               |  \n| **ICAM1**| Leukocyte adhesion molecule           | TNF-α Signaling               |  \n\n---\n\n### Prioritized Genes in Sarcomatoid Transformation  \n1. **SEMA3C**: Overexpressed in sRCC; semaphorins promote tumor angiogenesis and metastasis in other cancers (e.g., breast, prostate). May drive EMT via integrin signaling.  \n2. **DCBLD2**: Upregulated; though understudied, its role in cell growth inhibition suggests loss of growth control in sRCC.  \n3. **TFAP2A**: Transcription factor regulating EMT genes (e.g., E-cadherin repression). Overexpression may promote mesenchymal phenotype.  \n4. **NPR1**: Upregulated natriuretic receptor; natriuretic peptides inhibit tumor growth in RCC, suggesting NPR1 may act as a tumor suppressor.  \n5. **MOXD1**: Dopamine beta-hydroxylase activity; downregulation of catecholamine metabolism may alter tumor microenvironment.  \n\n**Note**: Prioritize_genes found no direct literature links to sRCC, indicating novel roles requiring validation.  \n\n---\n\n### Implications and Next Steps  \n1. **Therapeutic Relevance**: EMT markers (e.g., CD44, POSTN) may serve as targets for mesenchymal subtype-specific therapies.  \n2. **Immune Sensitivity**: Downregulation of TNF-α/NF-κB signaling may explain ICB responsiveness in sRCC, warranting immune microenvironment profiling.  \n3. **Validation Needs**: Functional studies on SEMA3C and TFAP2A in sRCC models to confirm EMT promotion.  \n\n**Next Steps**:  \n1. Validate EMT gene expression in spatial transcriptomic datasets.  \n2. Correlate TNF-α pathway downregulation with immune cell infiltration in sRCC.  \n3. Explore SEMA3C’s role in RCC cell migration/invasion assays.  \n\n--- \n\n**References**:  \n- Salgia et al. (2025) Cancer Cell (dataset source).  \n- EMT in cancer: Kalluri & Weinberg (2009) Nature Reviews Cancer.  \n- Natriuretic peptides in RCC: Felizola et al. (2014) Cancer Science.  \n\n*No PMIDs from prioritize_genes due to lack of direct literature links.*",
